BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-global-south-civil-society-microgrants-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: Global South Civil Society Microgrants 2
 026
DESCRIPTION:The Pulitzer Center funds civil society in the Global South to 
 build civic engagement projects around Pulitzer Center-supported journalis
 m: grassroots collectives\, community-led movements\, ARTISTS and civic co
 alitions taking published reporting to the communities it concerns. This g
 rant cycle is restricted to the Center's Climate and Environment topics: R
 AINFOREST (drivers of rainforest degradation\, the role of global markets\
 , finance and policy\, centring the rights and roles of Indigenous peoples
 )\, OCEAN (degradation of marine environments\, ocean health and the clima
 te crisis\, ocean governance\, science-backed and community-led solutions)
 \, and CLIMATE AND LABOR (the intersection of climate and labour\, includi
 ng gender\, livelihoods\, adaptation and community resilience). WHAT COUNT
 S AS A PROJECT: community engagement and organising that connects reportin
 g with audiences\; creative expressions\, explicitly including digital cam
 paigns\, public performances and creative publication formats\; public dia
 logues bringing together affected communities\, journalists\, decision-mak
 ers and academia\; and other innovative uses of the reporting that build o
 n local cultural practices. Every project must use at least one published 
 Pulitzer Center-supported reporting project meaningfully\, or involve a Pu
 litzer Center-supported journalist centrally\, and must name a clear targe
 t audience. OUT OF SCOPE: journalism and reporting projects themselves (th
 ose go to the Center's reporting grants)\, anything not built on Pulitzer 
 Center-supported reporting\, direct advocacy or lobbying\, campaigns endor
 sing political candidates or parties\, and for-profit projects. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: grassroots organisations\, community educators\, civil society organi
 sations and coalitions\, youth movements and other groups working at the i
 ntersection of journalism\, civic rights and active citizenship\, with pas
 t experience running civic engagement activities in the Global South\, pro
 ven experience on the named topics\, and the operational capacity to manag
 e a microgrant from an international organisation. APPLICATION: a project 
 description of no more than 400 words\, the target audience and desired im
 pact\, your methodology\, a timeline and budget\, the lead applicant's CV 
 with a letter of recommendation\, and\, where activities involve Indigenou
 s peoples or traditional communities\, a signed or recorded statement of c
 onsent from a community member. Applications are accepted in English\, Spa
 nish\, Portuguese\, French and Indonesian through Submittable. TIMELINE: a
 pplications 15 August to 6 September 2026\, selection announced mid-Septem
 ber 2026\, onboarding October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoca
 tion: Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and South
 east Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. Ci
 vil Society Microgrant instead.\nAward: USD 2\,000 to USD 4\,000 per proje
 ct. Applicants name the amount requested and submit a draft budget. Funds 
 are transferred to the partner after the agreement is signed\; partners fi
 le a financial narrative and outcomes report at the end but are NOT requir
 ed to collect detailed receipts. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to six 
 months of implementation\, October 2026 to March 2027\, with a virtual che
 ck-in in December 2026 and closure in early March 2027.\n\nMore: https://p
 ulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/global-south-
 civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/g
 lobal-south-civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and Sou
 theast Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. 
 Civil Society Microgrant instead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uj-artists-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: University of Johannesburg Artists in Residence 2027
DESCRIPTION:UJ Arts & Culture's annual residency gives artists a monthly st
 ipend and an institutional base at the University of Johannesburg for ten 
 months\, with the option of a remote rather than physical residency. Disci
 plines covered are visual and fine arts\, music\, theatre\, performance an
 d dance\, design\, film and television\, and literary arts. A virtual info
 rmation session with Pieter Jacobs\, Head of UJ Arts & Culture\, was sched
 uled for 17 August 2026 to walk applicants through the process. ELIGIBILIT
 Y: applicants must hold a relevant post-school qualification\, with prefer
 ence for master's and PhD holders\, and must not be affiliated with anothe
 r tertiary institution in South Africa. Preference has historically gone t
 o artists with African connections. APPLICATION: via the online form linke
 d from the UJ Arts & Culture residency page\; contact kmachoga@uj.ac.za or
  +27 11 559 1476 with questions. DEADLINE: 31 August 2026. No fee.\n\nOrga
 nisation: UJ Arts & Culture\, University of Johannesburg\nLocation: Johann
 esburg\, South Africa. Both physical and remote residencies can be applied
  for\, though office\, studio\, rehearsal and performance space is limited
  and not guaranteed.\nAward: Stipend of ZAR 12\,000 per month across a 10-
 month residency\, so roughly ZAR 120\,000 in total. No travel or accommoda
 tion budget on top of the stipend\, and studio or rehearsal space is limit
 ed rather than guaranteed. No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months during
  2027\n\nMore: https://arts.uj.ac.za/artists-in-residence/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arts.uj.ac.za/artists-in-residence/?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Johannesburg\, South Africa. Both physical and remote residencies 
 can be applied for\, though office\, studio\, rehearsal and performance sp
 ace is limited and not guaranteed.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:delfina-foundation-curators-central-west-africa-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Delfina Foundation: Open Call for Curators Based in Centr
 al and West Africa (London residency)
DESCRIPTION:With support from Delfina Foundation's Network of Patrons for A
 frica\, Delfina is offering a six- or twelve-week London residency within 
 its winter 2027 season to curators based in Central and West Africa. ELIGI
 BILITY: applicants must hold nationality and be based in one of Angola\, B
 enin\, Burkina Faso\, Burundi\, Cabo Verde\, Cameroon\, Central African Re
 public\, Chad\, Cote d'Ivoire\, Democratic Republic of the Congo\, Equator
 ial Guinea\, Gabon\, Ghana\, Guinea\, Guinea-Bissau\, Liberia\, Mali\, Mau
 ritania\, Niger\, Nigeria\, Republic of the Congo\, Sao Tome and Principe\
 , Senegal\, Sierra Leone\, The Gambia or Togo\; and must have at least fiv
 e years of professional curatorial practice in a freelance\, academic or i
 nstitutional context. THE PROGRAMME: alongside time and space to develop t
 heir work\, residents get guided visits to museums\, galleries\, non-profi
 ts\, artist-run spaces and studios\, plus trips to institutions outside Lo
 ndon\; opportunities to meet and engage with artists\, curators\, academic
 s and researchers through organised presentations\, studio visits\, events
  and meals\; professional development including presentations and crits\, 
 portfolio reviews and mentoring activities to strengthen their practice\; 
 and collaborative exchange between residents through informal gatherings a
 nd meals. APPLY via the application form on the open call page\; Delfina s
 trongly recommends reading the Guidance Notes document carefully before st
 arting. Deadline Sunday 30 August 2026\, 23:59 BST.\n\nOrganisation: Delfi
 na Foundation\nLocation: Delfina Foundation\, 29-31 Catherine Place\, Lond
 on\, UK. Applicants must hold nationality and be based in an eligible Cent
 ral or West African country.\nAward: Fully supported: a return economy-cla
 ss flight\, airport transfers in the UK\, reimbursement of visa fees if re
 quired\, a living allowance of GBP 40 per day\, a materials and research a
 llowance of GBP 70 per week\, a travel pass for London's public transport 
 network\, and a single bedroom with a desk at Delfina Foundation\, a house
  hosting up to eight residents with shared bathrooms\, laundry rooms\, kit
 chen\, library and dining room. Supported by Delfina Foundation's Network 
 of Patrons for Africa. No application fee.\nDuration: Six or twelve weeks 
 within Delfina's winter 2027 season\, which runs 5 January to 28 March 202
 7.\n\nMore: https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call
 -for-curators-based-in-central-and-west-africa/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call-for-cura
 tors-based-in-central-and-west-africa/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Delfina Foundation\, 29-31 Catherine Place\, London\, UK. Applican
 ts must hold nationality and be based in an eligible Central or West Afric
 an country.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fifm-atlas-workshops-post-production-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Marrakech IFF: Atlas Workshops 2026 (Films in Shooting or
  Post-Production)
DESCRIPTION:The Atlas Workshops are the Marrakech International Film Festiv
 al's industry and talent-development programme\, running since 2018 and no
 w in its 9th edition. This track is for feature films already in shooting 
 or post-production. Selected teams get personalised mentoring tailored to 
 each project across scriptwriting\, production\, distribution\, editing an
 d scoring\, plus access to the festival's industry network\, and compete f
 or cash prizes decided by a dedicated jury. ELIGIBILITY: directors and pro
 ducers who are from\, or in the diaspora of\, the Arab world or the Africa
 n continent\, working on their first\, second or third feature (fiction\, 
 documentary or animation)\, with a production company attached. Open acros
 s that whole region\, not restricted to Morocco. There is a separate Proje
 cts in Development track whose deadline (6 July 2026) has already passed f
 or this edition. Deadline 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Festival Intern
 ational du Film de Marrakech (Atlas Workshops)\nLocation: Online in Octobe
 r 2026\, then onsite in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-26 November 2026\, alongs
 ide the Marrakech International Film Festival.\nAward: No application fee.
  Participation in the online and onsite programme is covered. Cash prizes 
 are awarded by a dedicated jury at the close of the Workshops\; the festiv
 al does not publish the prize amounts on the call page.\nDuration: Five-da
 y online programme in October 2026\, followed by a five-day onsite program
 me 22-26 November 2026.\n\nMore: https://atlasateliers.marrakech-festival.
 com/en/call-for-applications?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
URL:https://atlasateliers.marrakech-festival.com/en/call-for-applications?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online in October 2026\, then onsite in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-2
 6 November 2026\, alongside the Marrakech International Film Festival.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fifm-atlas-station-producers-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Marrakech IFF: Atlas Station 2026 (Producers Track)
DESCRIPTION:Atlas Station is the Marrakech International Film Festival's pr
 ofessional development strand running alongside the Atlas Workshops\, aime
 d at building the Moroccan film industry. The producers track offers struc
 tured mentoring\, industry sessions and access to the festival's internati
 onal network during the 9th edition. ELIGIBILITY IS RESTRICTED: open to Mo
 roccan producers only\, not to international applicants. Deadline 31 Augus
 t 2026. See also the Atlas Station short film track (deadline 14 September
  2026) and the internationally open Atlas Workshops track for films in sho
 oting or post-production.\n\nOrganisation: Festival International du Film 
 de Marrakech (Atlas Workshops)\nLocation: Online in October 2026\, then on
 site in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-26 November 2026. Open to Moroccan produc
 ers only.\nAward: No application fee. Participation in the online and onsi
 te professional programme is covered.\nDuration: Five-day online programme
  in October 2026\, followed by a five-day onsite programme 22-26 November 
 2026.\n\nMore: https://atlasateliers.marrakech-festival.com/en/call-for-ap
 plications?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://atlasateliers.marrakech-festival.com/en/call-for-applications?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online in October 2026\, then onsite in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-2
 6 November 2026. Open to Moroccan producers only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:heart2world-book-prize-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260805
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:Deadline: Heart2World Publishing: Heart2World Book Prize 2026 (Inau
 gural\, Poetry)
DESCRIPTION:The inaugural Heart2World Book Prize\, from Heart2World Publish
 ing\, is a poetry prize for a full-length collection by a Nigerian writer.
  AWARD: NGN 250\,000 cash prize\, a full publishing and marketing package 
 (valued at approximately NGN 5 million)\, and 100 percent net royalties on
  the published collection. ELIGIBILITY: Nigerian citizens currently residi
 ng in Nigeria\; submit an unpublished poetry collection of 30 to 35 poems 
 written in English. There is no entry or reading fee (verified on the fund
 er page). DEADLINE: 5 August 2026. Apply via the prize page.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Heart2World Publishing\nLocation: Nigeria\; open to Nigerian writers c
 urrently residing in Nigeria.\nAward: NGN 250\,000 cash prize\, a full pub
 lishing and marketing package (valued at approximately NGN 5 million)\, an
 d 100 percent net royalties on the published collection. No entry or readi
 ng fee.\nDuration: One-off prize\; publication to follow\n\nMore: https://
 heart2worldpublishing.org/prize/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://heart2worldpublishing.org/prize/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Nigeria\; open to Nigerian writers currently residing in Nigeria.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:arua-early-career-research-fellowships-2026-option2@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
SUMMARY:Deadline: ARUA: Early-Career Research Fellowships 2026 (Option 2)
DESCRIPTION:An Early-Career Research Fellowship program funding up to 42 ea
 rly-career researchers to conduct globally competitive research at the hos
 t and affiliated institutions of ARUA's 13 Centres of Excellence and the 2
 2 Africa-Europe Clusters of Excellence. ELIGIBILITY: early-career research
 ers no older than 35 at the time of application\; at least 70 percent of f
 ellowships are reserved for female candidates. STRUCTURE: each fellowship 
 lasts six months\; Option 2 runs September 2026 to February 2027 (Option 1
 \, March to August 2026\, has closed). SUPPORT: monthly stipend of USD 2\,
 000\, a modest accommodation payment\, and travel support (return ticket t
 o the host institution)\; successful candidates are contracted by ARUA for
  six months. Deadline for Option 2: 15 July 2026.\n\nOrganisation: African
  Research Universities Alliance (ARUA)\, with the Mastercard Foundation an
 d Carnegie Corporation of New York\nLocation: Host and affiliated institut
 ions of ARUA's Centres of Excellence and Africa-Europe Clusters of Excelle
 nce\; for African early-career researchers\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 
 2\,000\, a modest accommodation payment\, and travel support (return ticke
 t to host institution). 6-month fellowship.\nDuration: 6 months (Option 2:
  September 2026 to February 2027)\n\nMore: https://arua.org/early-career-r
 esearch-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
URL:https://arua.org/early-career-research-fellowships/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Host and affiliated institutions of ARUA's Centres of Excellence a
 nd Africa-Europe Clusters of Excellence\; for African early-career researc
 hers
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cambridge-africa-alborada-research-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260905T174935Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260903
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cambridge-Africa: ALBORADA Research Fund 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Cambridge-Africa ALBORADA Research Fund competitively award
 s grants to pairs of researchers\, one from an African university or resea
 rch institution and one from the University of Cambridge or an allied rese
 arch institute\, across all disciplines\, to initiate and/or strengthen re
 search collaborations. ELIGIBILITY: both applicants must be at post-doctor
 al level or above\, with employment contracts extending beyond the award e
 nd date\, and apply with the support of their Head of Department or equiva
 lent\; the Cambridge applicant must work at the University of Cambridge or
  an affiliated institute (Wellcome Sanger Institute\, NIAB\, British Antar
 ctic Survey)\; the African applicant must be based at an African universit
 y or equivalent. Limited student support is considered where it enhances t
 he Cambridge-Africa relationship. FUNDING: GBP 1\,000 to 25\,000\, coverin
 g research costs including reagents\, fieldwork\, equipment and research-t
 raining activities in Africa. APPLY: the Cambridge-based applicant registe
 rs using an institutional email (cam.ac.uk\, sanger.ac.uk\, babraham.ac.uk
 \, bas.ac.uk or niab.com) and invites the Africa-based applicant via the o
 nline form. Deadline: 3 September 2026. Queries: alboradafund@cambridge-af
 rica.cam.ac.uk.\n\nOrganisation: Cambridge-Africa Programme\, University o
 f Cambridge (supported by The ALBORADA Trust)\nLocation: Pairs of research
 ers: one at the University of Cambridge or an allied institute (Wellcome S
 anger Institute\, NIAB\, British Antarctic Survey)\, one at an African uni
 versity or research institution\nAward: Competitive grants of GBP 1\,000 t
 o 25\,000. Covers research costs such as reagents\, fieldwork\, equipment 
 and research-training activities in Africa (e.g. setting up courses/worksh
 ops). No application fee.\nDuration: Short collaborative research projects
 \n\nMore: https://www.cambridge-africa.cam.ac.uk/initiatives/2026-call-for
 -applications?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.cambridge-africa.cam.ac.uk/initiatives/2026-call-for-applic
 ations?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Pairs of researchers: one at the University of Cambridge or an all
 ied institute (Wellcome Sanger Institute\, NIAB\, British Antarctic Survey
 )\, one at an African university or research institution
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
